{"kind":"artifact","text":"The architecture reference states the admitted-time authority rule and the boundary on what the browser may derive, and records this projection change as workroom-fold@14. Exact clean pushed head e1798050b37e8a3c48f985a672afc1628beff9a2 on request/decision-records-ui-v3; local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 487c41a9, checkout clean. Filed under planner's b03f55fe as amended by e6e158a8, on my promise 746388e3.\n\nWHAT THIS PAGE ADDS. The admitted-time authority rule: a ratification is decided by the satisfier on the target kind's definition as it stood when the target statement was admitted, never by whichever definition governs now — the rule lifecycle already follows, for the same reason. And the layer 5 to layer 7 boundary: layer 7 may read any field layer 5 projects and combine those fields for presentation, but may not name a relation layer 5 does not project and then treat it as a fact about the workroom. Adoption is the worked case.\n\nTHE PROFILE NUMBERS ON THIS PAGE ARE NOW TWO DIFFERENT FACTS, and keeping them apart is the point. Line 815 states that this projection change advances the profile to workroom-fold@14. Line 701 still states @13, which is main's guarded unclaimed-reassignment bump and is historically accurate — I left it alone rather than renumbering the record of a change that genuinely shipped as @13. Line 796 still states @12 for the older change. Renumbering any of those to match the newest would have made the page describe a history that did not happen.\n\nWHY @14 AT ALL. This lane bumped @12 to @13; while it was blocked, main independently bumped @12 to @13 at 4784ab98 for different projection bytes. Reusing @13 would let a cache written by main's shipped binary — carrying no Satisfier — pass the version gate, and every ratification would then be silently withheld from actors entitled to make it. Artifact 9b3f21bd records the collision and the four commits I checked it at.\n\nONE PASSAGE THIS LANE RELIES ON AND DID NOT WRITE. The page already states, at the live-membership boundary, that \"A departed actor may still supersede an earlier act they authored; that narrow cleanup exception confers no force on a new state or on a ratification.\" That sentence is load-bearing for the repair at d4748f59: it is why own-author withdraw stays outside the participant predicate while publish is inside it. I once described that withdraw as a false offer, contradicting this page; codex caught it and the page was right.\n\nCARRIED, NOT REWRITTEN. This page's content is the same material I carried by hand onto the plain-language rewrite when that merged as d8edc000a — main's rewrite intact, plus this lane's additions, front matter byte-identical, no citation added, removed or re-anchored. The only edit in this recut is the profile number at line 815.\n\nWHY THIS RESTS ON THE BEHAVIOUR ARTIFACTS. A documentation artifact resting only on the promise can never be flared by a change to the thing it describes. This one rests on internal/workroom 9b3f21bd and ui d4748f59, plus the live in-main predecessor at this path, so when that behaviour is succeeded this page is told.\n\nGATES RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok plus 1 no-test-files, zero failures and zero cached — which matters here because the docset gate caches past documentation edits and a cached pass would predate this change; gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0 with no output; npm test 99/99; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"e1798050b37e8a3c48f985a672afc1628beff9a2","path":"docs/reference/architecture.md"}}